<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huchuquan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 195–<a href="/years/216">216</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huchuquan was the last effectively independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a>, ruling from 195 to 216. In the wars at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> his people fought for and against various warlords until, in 216, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> detained him at Ye and divided the Southern Xiongnu into Five Divisions under Chinese supervision. His nephew Liu Bao's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a> later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a> state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Huchuquan was a younger brother of Yufuluo, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> chanyu whose family had been ousted from their domain by rebels among their own people. Yufuluo had come into conflict with the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> but appears to have surrendered to him shortly before his death in 195. Huchuquan then succeeded him as chanyu, a reign that ran from the late Eastern Han dynasty into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but at first he too remained in exile, around Hedong Commandery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Soon after his accession his uncle Qubei led their forces to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xian-of-han">Emperor Xian of Han</a> escape from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> received the emperor. Qubei later helped escort the emperor to Cao Cao's base at Xuchang. In 202 Huchuquan rebelled and allied himself with Yuan Shang, but after Cao Cao's officer Zhong Yao defeated him he surrendered.</p><p>In 206 Gao Gan, the warlord of Bing Province, close to defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, came in person to ask Huchuquan for help. He refused, and Gao Gan was later killed while fleeing towards Jing Province. In 216 Huchuquan went to the imperial court to pay homage to Emperor Xian. He was given an official rank and kept at Ye as an honoured prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>While Huchuquan stayed at Ye, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> rearranged what remained of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> into the Five Divisions and settled them around Taiyuan Commandery under the supervision of Qubei. Huchuquan kept the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>, now only a nominal one, until his death, and no new chanyu was proclaimed after him. With him the line of Southern Xiongnu chanyus came to an end.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huchuquan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He settled with his followers around Pingyang, east of the Fen River in Shanxi, where he died in 195 and was succeeded by his brother <b>Huchuquan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 216 Cao Cao detained the shanyu <b>Huchuquan</b> at Ye and divided the Southern Xiongnu into Five Divisions under Chinese supervision, ending the shanyu's independent authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a></li></ul></section>
